        <body>
            <div xml:id="d520">

                <pb facs="0947" n="885" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg885"/>

                <head>No. 515.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Maynard to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1878-06-05"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 5, 1878</date>.
                        (Received July 3.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 255.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to announce still another
                    change in the high office of Grand Vizier.</p>
                <p>Last night the imperial hatt was published at the Porte, proclaiming <pb
                        facs="0948" n="886" xml:id="pg886"/> His Highness Safvet Pasha, now and for
                    a long time past minister of foreign affairs, Grand Vizier, in place of His
                    Highness Mehemet Buchdi Pasha who had held the office just one week.</p>
                <p>Phosphor Mustapha Pasha is appointed minister of war in place of His Highness
                    Mahmond Dam ad Pasha, who received the office ten days ago.</p>
                <p>There is apparently little significance to be attached to these changes beyond
                    the distracted condition of the Ottoman Government.</p>
                <p>The caution and prudence of the new Grand Vizier will be more likely to prevent
                    mischief than to suggest remedies.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp; c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>HORACE MAYNARD.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
